The `mutable` keyword. Declaring a member variable `mutable` allows it to be modified even within a `const` member function. This is typically used for internal, non-observable state (like caching, logging, or counters) that doesn't change the logical state of the object as perceived by its users.
```cpp
class MyClass {
    int value;
    mutable int accessCount; // Can be modified in const functions
public:
    int getValue() const {
        accessCount++; // OK, accessCount is mutable
        return value;
    }
};
```
